  simple: "The Kingdom of Damot was a medieval kingdom located south of the Abay, the Blue Nile River. It was a powerful state that forced the Sultanate of Shewa to pay tribute and defeated the armies of the Zagwe dynasty that were sent to subdue it. Damot also conquered several Muslim and Christian territories. Because it was so strong, the Muslim state of Shewa and the new Christian state under Yekuno Amlak formed an alliance to counter its influence. Damot's history as an independent entity ended when King Amda Tsiyon subdued it in the 14th century, after which it remained under Solomonic influence. Later, under pressure of Oromo attacks, some of its people moved north of the Abay and settled in southern Gojjam.",
  detailed: "<p><b>A state south of the Abay.</b> The Kingdom of Damot was a medieval kingdom located south of the Abay, the Blue Nile River. Its position gave it control of lands beyond the great river that separated it from the northern Christian and the eastern Muslim states.</p><p><b>A powerful rival.</b> Damot was a powerful state that forced the Sultanate of Shewa to pay tribute. It also defeated the armies of the Zagwe dynasty that were sent to subdue its territory. Beyond defending itself, Damot conquered several Muslim and Christian territories, extending its influence over its neighbours.</p><p><b>The alliance against Damot.</b> Damot grew strong enough to threaten both of its larger neighbours. In response, the Muslim state of Shewa and the new Christian state under Yekuno Amlak formed an alliance to counter the influence of Damot in the region. This shows that common danger could bring together states of different religions.</p><p><b>Loss of independence.</b> Damot's history as an independent entity ended after King Amda Tsiyon subdued it in the 14th century, and it remained under the influence of the Solomonic dynasty. From then on it was no longer an independent power in the region.</p><p><b>Later migration.</b> Under the pressure of Oromo attacks, some sections of the people of Damot were forced to move north of the Abay and settled in southern Gojjam during the last quarter of the 16th and the early years of the 17th centuries. This migration shows how war and population movement reshaped settlement patterns in the region long after the kingdom itself had lost its independence.</p>",
